\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A twisted prism with a complex multiplicity block}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Take two triangles with vertices #{x,x'} indexed by
#{x\in\mathbb Z/3\mathbb Z}. Give the upper triangle weight
#{a=1}, the lower triangle weight #{b=2}, the edges #{\{x,x'\}}
weight #{u=1}, and the edges #{\{x,(x+1)'\}} weight #{v=1/2}.
The [graph precision](fdip-000A) is #{Q=L+I}.
Each edge is undirected for the energy; arbitrary chosen orientations
are transported with the [signed convention](fdip-004S).}

\p{The simultaneous rotation #{x\mapsto x+1} generates #{C_3}.
It is the entire weight-preserving vertex automorphism group.
The weighted degrees #{7/2} and #{11/2} distinguish the two layers.
The interlayer edges of weight #{1} force the same permutation
#{\pi} on both layers, and those of weight #{1/2} force
#{\pi(x+1)=\pi(x)+1}. Thus only the three rotations remain.}

\p{The real representation is
#{\mathbb R^6\cong\mathbb R^{\oplus2}\oplus W^{\oplus2}},
where #{W} is the two-dimensional rotation representation and has
#{D=\mathbb C} by \ref{fdip-0051}. The commutant is
#{M_2(\mathbb R)\times M_2(\mathbb C)}, of real dimension
#{4+8=12}. Its orbitals are the four choices of ordered layers
and three differences #{y-x}. The symmetric dimension is
#{3+4=7}: there are two diagonal orbits, two within-layer edge
orbits and three interlayer pair orbits.}

\p{Use normalized constant vectors on the two layers for the
trivial sector. For the other sector put
#{\zeta=e^{2\pi i/3}} and represent its upper values by
#{f(x)=\sqrt{2/3}\operatorname{Re}(z\zeta^{-x})}, and its lower
values by the same formula with #{z'}.
This is an isometry from #{\mathbb C^2} with its real Euclidean
inner product to the four-dimensional sector. Evaluating the edge
energy gives
##{\begin{aligned}
Q_{\mathrm{triv}}&=
 \begin{pmatrix}5/2&-3/2\\-3/2&5/2\end{pmatrix},\\
Q_{\mathbb C}&=
 \begin{pmatrix}
11/2&-3/4+i\sqrt3/4\\
-3/4-i\sqrt3/4&17/2
\end{pmatrix}.
\end{aligned}}
Indeed the diagonal Fourier entries are #{1+3a+u+v} and
#{1+3b+u+v}; the upper-right entry is
#{-u-v\zeta^{-1}}. The trivial eigenvalues are #{1,4}.
The complex block has trace #{14}, determinant #{46}, and eigenvalues
#{7-\sqrt3,7+\sqrt3}, each repeated twice over the reals. Hence
##{\det_{\mathbb R}Q=4\cdot46^2=8464.}
The square is required by the [real determinant convention](fdip-0050).}

\p{The imaginary entry records the shift in the declared Fourier
coordinates. Rephasing one coordinate can make this single
Hermitian matrix real; the intrinsic complex feature is the full
commutant #{M_2(\mathbb C)}, not an obstruction to diagonalizing
one positive operator. This calculation adapts the cyclic Fourier
modes of \ref{fdip-004W}, based on
\citet{Section 5.1, p. 35}{serre1977linear}.}
